-- title:
--   Blocks of side at least one are nonempty
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $\ell \in \mathbb{R}$ with $\ell \ge 1$, and let $c \in \mathbb{R}^d$. With
--   $$Q_\ell(c) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \bar A_\ell(c), \qquad \bar A_\ell(c) = \Bigl\{y : \lVert y - c\rVert_\infty \le \tfrac{\ell}{2}\Bigr\}, \qquad \lVert u\rVert_\infty = \sup_i |u_i|,$$
--   the block of side $\ell$ centred at $c$ (Definition 5.10), we have
--
--   $$Q_\ell(c) \neq \emptyset .$$
--
--   The bound $\ell \ge 1$ is what guarantees that a cube of side $\ell$ is wide enough to capture at least one point of the underlying unit lattice: each coordinate interval $[c_i - \ell/2,\, c_i + \ell/2]$ has length $\ell \ge 1$ and therefore contains an integer. Nonemptiness is needed wherever a *representative* of a block must be chosen: the walks of the cyclic assembly scheme pick one point per block and run it along an entire walk of blocks, which is only possible if no block along the way is empty.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The hypothesis is a genuine lower bound on the side, not merely nonnegativity: for $0 \le \ell < 1$ the block may well be empty, which is why finiteness (proved under $\ell \ge 0$) and nonemptiness carry different hypotheses.
